---
/** Method sheet for The Invoked Past (Werkgruppe §3.5 duty). Plain English, static. */
import data from '@/data/invoked/latest.json'
import type { InvokedData } from '@/lib/invoked/types'
import { count, percent, standoutWindow } from '@/lib/invoked/format'

const d = data as unknown as InvokedData
const neighbourhood = standoutWindow(d.method.standout)
const ceiling = d.stats.max_year_observed
const rules: [string, string][] = [
  ['a — year window', d.method.rules.a_year_window],
  ['b — self-reference', d.method.rules.b_self_reference],
  ['c — per-article dedup', d.method.rules.c_per_article_dedup],
  ['d — stoplist', d.method.rules.d_stoplist],
]
const removed = Object.entries(d.stats.mentions_removed)
const resolutions = Object.entries(d.method.resolution_codes)
---

<article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-14">
  <header class="mb-10">
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">Method sheet</p>
    <h1 class="mt-1 text-3xl font-bold tracking-[-0.02em]">The Invoked Past</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-fg-muted">
      Method {d.method.version} since {d.method.since}. Spec and calibration record:
      <code class="text-xs">docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-14-the-invoked-past-design.md</code> in the site repository.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">Source and field</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      The last 24 hours of GDELT's raw 15-minute GKG 2.1 files ({d.stats.slots_expected} static downloads from
      data.gdeltproject.org — no API, no key; the shared fetch layer The Balance already uses). The sensor is
      <code class="text-xs">{d.method.field}</code>: for every article, the dates its text refers to, each as a
      block of <code class="text-xs">resolution#month#day#year#offset</code>. The field was added to GKG 2.1 in
      2015 explicitly for anniversary analysis. The instrument reads it; it does not re-implement it.
      {d.method.stream}.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-line text-left font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-fg-faint">
            <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Resolution code</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Meaning</th>
            <th class="py-2 text-right font-medium">mentions today</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {resolutions.map(([code, meaning]) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-line/50">
              <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 font-mono text-xs">{code}</td>
              <td class="py-1.5 pr-3">{meaning.replace(/_/g, ' ')}</td>
              <td class="py-1.5 text-right font-mono text-xs tabular-nums">{count(d.stats.resolutions[meaning] ?? 0)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      The codes are the ones measured against live data, not the ones a document promised. Only mentions that
      resolve to a year enter the histogram; a month and a day without a year cannot be placed on the axis at
      all and is removed by rule (a) — counted, like every removal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">The four cleaning rules (versioned)</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-line text-left font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-fg-faint">
            <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Rule</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-medium">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rules.map(([name, text]) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-line/50">
              <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 whitespace-nowrap font-mono text-xs">{name}</td>
              <td class="py-1.5 text-fg-muted">{text}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      Every rule reports what it removed, in every day file: today
      {removed.map(([key, n], i) => (
        <>{i > 0 ? ' · ' : ' '}<code class="text-xs">{key}</code> {count(n)}</>
      ))}
      of {count(d.stats.mentions_raw)} raw mentions, leaving {count(d.stats.mentions_kept)}. The v1 stoplist is
      empty and published as empty: the false-positive review before launch found no systematic offender, and a
      cleaning rule nobody can inspect is not a method, it is a habit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">The finding: the standout, not the maximum</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      {d.method.standout}
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      In full: for every year with at least 30 mentions, the instrument takes the median of the
      ±{neighbourhood} years around it (that year excluded), and scores the excess over that baseline in units
      of its square root — a Poisson-style surprise. A year must also reach at least twice its neighbourhood,
      so volume alone cannot win; ties break to the earlier year, so the rule is total and the result is
      reproducible from the committed file. A plain ratio was tested first and rejected: it crowns whatever
      sits in the thinnest neighbourhood, which on the calibration day meant a year whose 73 mentions were 65
      from one country.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">The {ceiling} wall</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      {d.method.inherited_ceiling}
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      Practically: the most-invoked year is structurally the ceiling year, the youngest age bucket is
      structurally empty, and both say so wherever they appear. The figure draws the missing stretch as an
      empty hatched band, because a gap that is not drawn reads as a press that stopped remembering.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">The register, and why it waits</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      The decay law (Candia et&nbsp;al. 2019) is the test hypothesis, and testing it needs a series of tracked
      events. The register of {d.method.register_size} events is founded from the first
      {' '}{d.method.register_min_days} archived days — never from a single day, because this instrument's first
      run fell on 15 August and a one-day rule would have frozen one country's anniversaries into it as though
      they were the world's canon. The founding window is then excluded from any fit by its own selection
      effect (those dates were chosen <em>because</em> they led that window), and the series starts the day
      after founding. Until then <code class="text-xs">law_test</code> ships as <code class="text-xs">pending</code>,
      naming what it still lacks — a number produced from one day would be theatre.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">Known limits, disclosed</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      <li>The date extraction is GDELT's, and every error mode of it is inherited; the four rules trim, they do not repair.</li>
      <li>An invoked year is not an invoked event: 1989 carries Tiananmen and the Wall in one bar. Naming events is the register's job, and the register waits for an archive.</li>
      <li>A year carried overwhelmingly by one country is as often an extraction artefact as a memory — hence the surprise score instead of a ratio, and the published country share beside every finding.</li>
      <li>English-monitored stream only: anniversaries observed mainly in other languages are underrepresented. Translation stream = named v2.</li>
      <li>Source country is the country of the newsroom, from GDELT's domain lookup ({percent(d.stats.articles_country_mapped / Math.max(1, d.stats.articles_scanned))} of today's articles mapped) — never the country a past belongs to.</li>
      <li>Per-1,000 rates are per article scanned, not per article carrying a date ({percent(d.stats.articles_with_dates_share)} of articles carry one), so the denominator stays the same on every day and comparisons across days survive.</li>
      <li>No baseline yet: day-against-normal scores need an archive, and this one starts accumulating with the file this page is built from.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">Archive and reproducibility</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      One JSON per day under <code class="text-xs">src/data/invoked/</code>, committed nightly by the
      counter-measurement workflow; committed day files are immutable — the pipeline refuses to overwrite them,
      and there is no backfill. Failures surface as disclosed gaps (“finding omitted”), never as quieter-looking
      days. Every day file carries its own top exact dates, so the register's founding is reproducible by anyone
      from the committed archive alone. Pipeline: <code class="text-xs">pipelines/invoked/refresh.py</code>,
      standard library only.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="border-t border-line pt-5 text-sm text-fg-muted">
    <p>
      Data © <a href="https://www.gdeltproject.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">The GDELT Project</a>,
      used and republished with attribution per its terms of use. Daily archive JSONs: CC0.
      Back to <a href="/invoked-past" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">the instrument</a>.
    </p>
  </footer>
</article>
